      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T16:39:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If memory allocation in `__topup_hyp_memcache()` fails, it will return -ENOMEM. Will this cause dma_map_sg() to fail while it should succeed?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7395f127_a8db72f6",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1965911
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T16:54:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If `__topup_hyp_memcache` fails with `ENOMEM` (which is unlikely and means the system is in very bad state already) `__pkvm_topup_hyp_alloc_mgt_flags` would fail which makes `kvm_arm_smmu_topup_memcache` to fail,  which should eventually fail the `iommu_map_sg`, that should be the expected or am I missing something?\n\n\nhttps://android.googlesource.com/kernel/common/+/refs/heads/android15-6.6/drivers/iommu/iommu.c#2636",
      "parentUuid": "0fc022fb_98892619",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b6c2f379_e6dbadcc",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T17:04:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is it acceptable to make `dma_map_sg()` fail if not enough memory is available? As one can see in `__dwc3_ep0_do_control_data()`, it does not expect `dma_map_sg()` to fail. If DMA mapping fails, the following code is triggered and the error is not propagated:\n```\n\t\tif (ret)\n\t\t\treturn;\n```",
      "parentUuid": "7395f127_a8db72f6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cc59718b_136afe21",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150933
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T17:26:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The documentation for `dma_map_sg_attrs` reads:\n\n\"Zero is returned for any error.\"\n\nIf the DWC3 driver doesn\u0027t handle this appropriately, then that\u0027s an issue with the DWC3 driver. (From what I can see, it does run `dev_err(dev, \"failed to map SGs\\n\");`)\n\nPretty much every IOMMU driver has this challenge. This driver is no different.",
      "parentUuid": "b6c2f379_e6dbadcc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bb65a099_b50f24e8",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T17:35:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since `dma_map_sg_attrs()` returns zero upon error, it is not possible for the caller to tell whether the error is retryable or not. All drivers I\u0027m familiar with give up if `dma_map_sg_attrs()` fails and do not retry. Hence, I think that the IOMMU code that is called from inside `dma_map_sg_attrs()` should not fail if the caller passes valid arguments to `dma_map_sg_attrs()`. I do not agree that the dwc3 driver should retry if `dma_map_sg_attrs()` fails because that driver cannot know whether the error is retryable or not.",
      "parentUuid": "cc59718b_136afe21",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d7ecaeaf_05f41ffa",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150933
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T22:02:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not suggesting that the dwc3 driver should retry. I guess it should probably roll back any setup that has already been done and then propagate the error back to `usb_ep_queue`.\n\n`dma_map_sg_attrs()` might fail for a variety of reasons including out-of-memory situations, exhaustion of I/O virtual address space and DMA buffers being inaccessible due to limited addressing capabilities of a device (i.e. too narrow DMA mask).\n\nI don\u0027t see how we could possibly make `iommu_map_sg` succeed if the driver can\u0027t allocate memory for a required translation table. Every IOMMU driver I looked at has this challenge. Even `vmap` can run into this issue when it fails to allocate a translation table.\n\nTo your question: Yes, `dma_map_sg()` will fail if this IOMMU driver can\u0027t allocate memory for new translation tables that are required as a result of a mapping request.",
      "parentUuid": "bb65a099_b50f24e8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f024b823_bd7dbd2b",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 95,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-18T22:07:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The only solution I can think of is to allocate memory from a non-atomic context before it is needed in an atomic context. An interesting text about the DMA map and unmap calls is this patch series cover letter: Michael Kelley, [[RFC 0/7] Introduce swiotlb throttling](https://lore.kernel.org/all/20240822183718.1234-1-mhklinux@outlook.com/), August 2024.",
      "parentUuid": "d7ecaeaf_05f41ffa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 10
      },
      "revId": "efbeefd2fc7aedac25b6871c39725491c665dd8f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1d0d5d72_f6b22f83",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu-v3/arm-smmu-v3-kvm.c",
